This part can be relatively easy but since you mention Windows below -
getting unrestricted access to the program's input & output may be
difficult without patching. I'll let others comment on this step if
you must use Windows.

On Linux / Unix, this step can be easier - a "pseudo terminal" (pty)
is one way to implement that in a manner that requires no changes to
nethack. Others may mention applications that capture the output of
nethack - that is extremely easy on Linux / Unix. Its the input side
that I have found to be somewhat problematic to implement reliably (if
you don't use a pty) without causing other problems.

> while 1:
>    get screen output from nethack
It turns out this step has a lot of special cases. Examples include:
 o prompting when you start nethack (to select a character, etc.)
 o current cursor position is on the "@" [a somewhat reliable
indicator that nethack is waiting for input but not always]; invisible
players without "see invisible" can have a problem with this step
 o -- More -- processing
Properly parsing ANSI terminal codes (most likely for painting the
screen) is an additional complication. I believe Rog-O-Matic
(described below) redefined the terminal control codes to a much
simpler set.

>    process output
You mention AI in other responses - I assume you know how to do this
step.

>    feed keystroke to nethack
Relatively easy if you have direct control of the nethack I/O.

